{"entryId":"1715","paragraphs":[{"sourceId":"heilprin:sefaria-warsaw:ta:1715:1","sourceSha256":"931abb50906e0d8736ca52a16fa8f2eda40dd00fe12ac15b329c765216382187","englishSha256":"f603adbdce34dfbde9630e3b14384d9c88e1def43ce644d2629c459c0e40ba72","citations":[{"id":"1715:1:citation-01","he":[38,75],"en":[161,169],"target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Yoma_81b.8"},"reference":"Yoma 81b:8","assessment":"Yoma supplies the exact expression in a discussion of ginger. The attached Rashi and geographical gloss are separate checks.","evidence":[{"reference":"Yoma 81b:8","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Yoma_81b.8"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"}]},{"id":"1715:1:citation-02","he":[76,91],"en":[207,221],"target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Rashi_on_Yoma_81b.8.2"},"reference":"Rashi on Yoma 81b:8:2","assessment":"The Yoma Rashi gives the exact expression “from the land of Cush.” It is the commentator’s geography.","evidence":[{"reference":"Rashi on Yoma 81b:8:2","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Rashi_on_Yoma_81b.8.2"},"witness":"Vilna Edition"}]},{"id":"1715:1:citation-03","he":[93,124],"en":[248,270],"target":{"entry":3351,"paragraph":1},"reference":"Seder HaDorot, Tanaim and Amoraim 3351:1","assessment":"In Yevamot, Shmuel bar Yehudah calls himself a convert. Rashi says he and his father converted. Rabbeinu Tam, as cited by Tosafot, identifies the father as Yehudah Hindua. Treat this as a reported identification alongside Kiddushin’s statement that Yehudah had no heirs.","evidence":[{"reference":"Seder HaDorot 3351:1","target":{"entry":3351,"paragraph":1},"witness":"Pinned Warsaw digital witness"},{"reference":"Kiddushin 22b:19","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Kiddushin_22b.19"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"},{"reference":"Yevamot 101b:16","target":{"url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Yevamot_101b.16"},"witness":"William Davidson Edition - Vocalized Aramaic"},{"reference":"Rashi on Yevamot 101b:16